#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "e7ff1089-5799-5654-8e78-5c801a676aa1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6167" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0} \\:\\dfrac{1}{4 \\cos \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\cos 0} = \\dfrac14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078299 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "7a06800b-021c-5e24-b25b-e0026730165a" :question/math-academy-id "e-94" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x \\to 0,$ both the numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{x} = 1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87a6ab7-70dd-56fb-875c-f3df68c06b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317501} {:answer/id #uuid "0c075e91-d394-5778-9998-63a0666727cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317502} {:answer/id #uuid "34260e19-3634-5f6f-aa69-2cbbfb984753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317503} {:answer/id #uuid "c25786cc-e74a-50fb-a370-24f92618d318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317504} {:answer/id #uuid "bb084e61-5b05-579c-b38b-cb7e2fa6562e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34260e19-3634-5f6f-aa69-2cbbfb984753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317503} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9bcb693-b589-5999-9b77-e0e7d238488f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0552357-0b1d-5f8f-a303-1bf9280ab97c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105433" :question/problem "${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{4\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )=4{cos}^{2}⁡0=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =1\\cdot 4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3e7dbd9-c79e-58b1-9e64-605b1dc9c9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e7dbd9-c79e-58b1-9e64-605b1dc9c9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317508} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef76868-ef40-5f35-9a59-4dc4a13702e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f76e3acd-08d5-5684-893a-f2e7ab95cc88" :question/math-academy-id "q-302820" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2x}{x}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2+3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (2+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee42bc5-fa4b-551c-bfeb-27613fe50f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317511} {:answer/id #uuid "2950a4c5-4cd2-5fdf-83a1-9e5ed2a6947c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee42bc5-fa4b-551c-bfeb-27613fe50f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317511} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cdbd09-f4a6-5047-95aa-51ace608e3b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3808e545-f492-5548-9d11-622244ade519" :question/math-academy-id "q-339797" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351c6eb4-27a9-54ef-b1ff-bf8d42724892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317515} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea83414-16c9-5eb3-a3ee-b603b8a14283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea83414-16c9-5eb3-a3ee-b603b8a14283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317516} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c52e3a-3acd-5c9b-a8b5-3b9f8882d22e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f9b4741-0192-5eb6-b5d9-f7b271ca5682" :question/math-academy-id "q-340124" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3812b-a432-5f17-b78b-08135e18dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d3812b-a432-5f17-b78b-08135e18dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317519} :answer-field/id #uuid "84fbab3d-df13-55e6-9bee-693cbdd829aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "186dc72e-1eb6-57fe-a676-4e381bb03180" :question/math-academy-id "q-340150" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787b51ce-a232-5f43-b96b-c5f064aa65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787b51ce-a232-5f43-b96b-c5f064aa65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab70b2b-3514-54a5-9ca2-3136cee1ac22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e2979d7-cf77-55ac-87de-5e64b9f02798" :question/math-academy-id "q-340672" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}({\\theta }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837e6fbe-fd90-5bd4-953d-21b234f3313a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317525} {:answer/id #uuid "c11970b8-3b2f-508c-a275-c5366532def1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317526} {:answer/id #uuid "fa90a7de-3ab2-55cd-b3b9-d4f01788f2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317527} {:answer/id #uuid "c68cfc2f-eb03-53a7-95ba-f090198ae3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317528} {:answer/id #uuid "d21b60d6-2a35-5f11-b6be-40f7cc60247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c11970b8-3b2f-508c-a275-c5366532def1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317526} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf8a111-c166-5c94-8106-de3c494a4b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0082f13b-a36e-5608-84a0-d88b4a772146" :question/math-academy-id "q-68213" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2x}{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1233889c-15f0-522e-931b-36e2e5990ce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317532} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8f65fb-02d6-539d-8195-ffd5a14d005d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317533} {:answer/id #uuid "64fb49f4-2923-5682-b4ef-3e5d41abe167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317534} {:answer/id #uuid "66c6731f-7c24-5ab4-8f6f-66f1cae88518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317535} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd82b22-a513-5d5d-b36e-76d10a005f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c6731f-7c24-5ab4-8f6f-66f1cae88518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317535} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac48ec8f-e3f6-5018-b502-da715868a275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab8d34aa-82cf-59f7-b4b9-bc5dd0483474" :question/math-academy-id "q-68215" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e686ea1b-94bd-5dad-93e5-9bd7b338e021" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce28f2b1-3019-5500-b7f2-c5f239fff423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539} {:answer/id #uuid "810984a0-1091-5a7a-a2fe-bf08e084fc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317540} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f36692-0b4f-5c83-876e-6760bd89d464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317541} {:answer/id #uuid "545e9749-eac0-5c5e-a70a-28de85d98e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317542} {:answer/id #uuid "a048d56d-5eca-58be-a964-f7719898380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a048d56d-5eca-58be-a964-f7719898380a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8204f1-1402-5ecd-8279-e75270a0cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e061e9cc-d750-5748-977f-50038cee6fb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73539" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x})=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b9df69a-3072-5131-a5c8-64c1744abcbd" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit of a Function Involving the Special Limit With Sine: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5f3ef3b-f2a7-5831-ad69-d73bd2d530cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317546} {:answer/id #uuid "32ba09f3-6f25-5996-9334-d05dfa3db2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317547} {:answer/id #uuid "3924e8e7-6041-5061-ab2b-1afb4b5d761b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317548} {:answer/id #uuid "d49244c3-e3d8-5b30-a8f9-4c854c57370c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317549} {:answer/id #uuid "79368d3f-2f5c-5e5c-a102-1956aaf64842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3924e8e7-6041-5061-ab2b-1afb4b5d761b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317548} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20bb663-9eed-5948-8df3-b03b23b904f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc0eece6-a40f-5cd4-a384-c74d156a2371" :question/math-academy-id "q-73546" :question/problem "Find the value of ${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
